WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.990
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.932
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.232 --> 00:00:15.449
Unit twelve: Critical Thinking Frameworks. Today you will express degrees of possibility with modal verbs.

4
00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.169
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.773
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:24.885
Critical thinking.

7
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:30.321
Critical thinking means the disciplined analysis and evaluation of information.

8
00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:33.857
Critical thinking is a defence against misinformation.

9
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.358
Your turn. Say: Critical thinking.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.515
Critical thinking.

11
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.274
Assumption.

12
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:47.723
Assumption means a belief accepted as true without proof.

13
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:52.313
The model rests on a hidden assumption about human behaviour.

14
00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:55.209
Your turn. Say: Assumption.

15
00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.114
Assumption.

16
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:00.863
Evidence.

17
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.312
Evidence means information that supports or refutes a claim.

18
00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:09.391
Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence.

19
00:01:09.983 --> 00:01:12.670
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

20
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:16.685
Evidence.

21
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:18.509
Perspective.

22
00:01:19.517 --> 00:01:22.561
Perspective means a particular way of viewing an issue.

23
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.102
Including rural perspectives changed the conclusion.

24
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:29.590
Your turn. Say: Perspective.

25
00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:33.516
Perspective.

26
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.530
Evaluation.

27
00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:40.722
Evaluation means a systematic judgement of quality or significance.

28
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:44.489
Rigorous evaluation of sources takes practice.

29
00:01:45.083 --> 00:01:47.933
Your turn. Say: Evaluation.

30
00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:52.058
Evaluation.

31
00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:53.690
Bias.

32
00:01:54.683 --> 00:01:58.146
Bias means a tendency to favour certain conclusions unfairly.

33
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:02.131
Confirmation bias leads us to ignore contrary evidence.

34
00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:05.253
Your turn. Say: Bias.

35
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.078
Bias.

36
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:10.725
Reasoning.

37
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:15.260
Reasoning means the process of drawing conclusions logically.

38
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.848
Her reasoning was sound, but her data was outdated.

39
00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.033
Your turn. Say: Reasoning.

40
00:02:24.867 --> 00:02:25.775
Reasoning.

41
00:02:26.517 --> 00:02:27.402
Framework.

42
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:32.692
Framework means a structured set of steps or criteria for analysis.

43
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:36.281
The framework asks four questions about every claim.

44
00:02:36.883 --> 00:02:39.420
Your turn. Say: Framework.

45
00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:43.128
Framework.

46
00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:47.747
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:53.453
Visal, your essay argues that homework should be banned. What evidence did you use?

48
00:02:54.550 --> 00:03:01.359
Mostly my own experience. But I realise now that an unexamined assumption might weaken your argument.

49
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.229
Which assumption, exactly?

50
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:13.658
That all students experience homework the same way. Students without a quiet place to study may suffer more, while others could benefit.

51
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.564
So how will you revise the essay?

52
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:27.233
I'll apply a framework: identify assumptions, look for counter-evidence, and consider other perspectives. My conclusion may well change.

53
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.924
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:33.630
Grammar focus: Modal verbs for possibility.

55
00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.106
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.817 --> 00:03:38.803
An unexamined assumption might weaken your argument.

57
00:03:39.717 --> 00:03:41.983
Some students could benefit from homework.

58
00:03:42.883 --> 00:03:44.802
My conclusion may well change.

59
00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:55.620
Might, may and could express possibility, from less to more likely in context. Add 'well' ('may well') to signal a stronger possibility. All are followed by the bare infinitive.

60
00:03:56.383 --> 00:04:00.847
Be careful! Don't say: This assumption might to weaken the case.

61
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.299
Say: This assumption might weaken the case.

62
00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:07.923
After 'might', use the bare infinitive.

63
00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:11.328
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:13.773
Questioning Our Own Thinking.

65
00:04:14.073 --> 00:04:20.566
Critical thinking is often described as evaluating other people's arguments, yet its hardest task lies closer to home.

66
00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:26.573
An unexamined assumption might weaken your argument long before any opponent notices it.

67
00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:33.223
Confirmation bias, for instance, may lead us to accept evidence that flatters our existing beliefs.

68
00:04:34.233 --> 00:04:40.575
Structured frameworks counter this tendency by forcing us to search for disconfirming evidence and alternative perspectives.

69
00:04:41.567 --> 00:04:47.467
Applied consistently, such habits could transform not only our essays but also our decisions.

70
00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:52.232
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:53.083 --> 00:04:55.942
An unexamined assumption might blank your argument.

72
00:04:56.242 --> 00:04:59.663
A, weakens. B, to weaken. C, weaken.

73
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.072
The answer is C: weaken.

74
00:05:06.217 --> 00:05:08.739
Which expresses the strongest possibility?

75
00:05:09.039 --> 00:05:14.167
A, It might change.. B, It may well change.. C, It could possibly change..

76
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.373
The answer is B: It may well change..

77
00:05:21.517 --> 00:05:25.571
The tendency to favour evidence that supports your beliefs is blank bias.

78
00:05:25.871 --> 00:05:30.221
A, confirmation. B, sampling. C, selection.

79
00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:35.801
The answer is A: confirmation.

80
00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:40.169
A belief accepted as true without proof is an blank.

81
00:05:40.469 --> 00:05:44.366
A, evaluation. B, assumption. C, inference.

82
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.422
The answer is B: assumption.

83
00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:55.835
According to the reading, where does critical thinking's hardest task lie?

84
00:05:56.135 --> 00:06:02.041
A, in judging opponents. B, in examining our own thinking. C, in memorising frameworks.

85
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.470
The answer is B: in examining our own thinking.

86
00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:13.132
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.983 --> 00:06:16.888
What assumption might you need to question in your own thinking?

88
00:06:33.683 --> 00:06:35.184
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:35.484 --> 00:07:08.051
One assumption I might need to question is that hard work always leads to success. It is a motivating belief, but it may ignore factors such as family income or access to good teachers. For example, two equally hardworking students could achieve very different results in different schools. Of course, effort still matters enormously, so I would not abandon the belief entirely. Rather, I should hold it more carefully and recognise its limits.

90
00:07:08.351 --> 00:07:10.060
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:15.599
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal verbs for possibility.

92
00:07:15.899 --> 00:07:20.525
Today's value is self-awareness. Know your strengths and keep growing.

93
00:07:20.825 --> 00:07:23.698
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.998 --> 00:07:26.302
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

